\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Geometry of the 2D Cartesian floating-body problem. A flat-bottomed rigid body of half-width $a$ and mass-per-unit-length $m$, centred at $x = x_c$, rests on a viscous fluid layer of depth $D$ in a periodic domain of length $L$. The free surface $\eta (x, t)$ (blue) deforms continuously outside the wetted region $\mathcal  {B}$ and is pinned to the body bottom, $\eta = z_b(t)$, inside $\mathcal  {B}$. The surface pressure $p_s(x, t)$ (red, upward) is supported only on $\mathcal  {B}$: it is the Lagrange multiplier that transmits the fluid traction to the body and enforces the no-penetration condition $\partial _z\phi + w^3 = v_b$ on $\mathcal  {B}$. Capillary forces at the two contact lines $x = x_c \pm a$ (magenta) contribute a net vertical lifting force of $2\sigma \sin (\arctan (\eta _x))$ to the body equation of motion (Section~\ref {sec:explicit_traction}).}}{9}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:schematic_2D}{{1}{9}{Geometry of the 2D Cartesian floating-body problem. A flat-bottomed rigid body of half-width $a$ and mass-per-unit-length $m$, centred at $x = x_c$, rests on a viscous fluid layer of depth $D$ in a periodic domain of length $L$. The free surface $\eta (x, t)$ (blue) deforms continuously outside the wetted region $\mathcal {B}$ and is pinned to the body bottom, $\eta = z_b(t)$, inside $\mathcal {B}$. The surface pressure $p_s(x, t)$ (red, upward) is supported only on $\mathcal {B}$: it is the Lagrange multiplier that transmits the fluid traction to the body and enforces the no-penetration condition $\partial _z\phi + w^3 = v_b$ on $\mathcal {B}$. Capillary forces at the two contact lines $x = x_c \pm a$ (magenta) contribute a net vertical lifting force of $2\sigma \sin (\arctan (\eta _x))$ to the body equation of motion (Section~\ref {sec:explicit_traction})}{figure.1}{}}
